{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "בחדש השלישי", :translit "b'chodesh hash'lishi", :gloss "In the third month"} {:he "הגיורת השבויה המשוחררת", :translit "hagiyoret hash'vuyah ham'shuchraret", :gloss "the captive convert woman who is freed"} {:he "לא ינשאו עד שיהים להם ג' חדשים", :translit "lo yena'eu ad sheyihyeh lahem gimmel chodashim", :gloss "they may not be married until three months have passed for them"} {:he "ליום מתן תורה", :translit "l'yom matan torah", :gloss "until the day of the giving of the Torah"} {:he "שנתחתנו בו להקב\"ה", :translit "shenit'chat'nu bo lahakadosh baruch hu", :gloss "on which they were betrothed to HaShem blessed be He"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Halakhic analogy: Israel's three-month wait parallels the convert's waiting period."}], :frame {:text "A captive woman who converts must wait three months before marriage so that any existing pregnancy can be identified (Yevamot 42b). The Talmud derives this from Israel's three months in the wilderness before Sinai: they left Egypt in Nisan and received the Torah in Sivan, a three-month interval corresponding to the convert's waiting period (Yevamot 42a). The reception of the Torah at Sinai is cast as Israel's betrothal to God (Shabbat 82a describes Sinai as the betrothal; see also Exodus Rabbah 41:6).", :sources ["Yevamot 42a" "Yevamot 42b" "Shabbat 82a" "Exodus Rabbah 41:6"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לצאת", :translit "l'tzeit", :gloss "to go out"} {:he "לצאת בני ישראל", :translit "l'tzeit b'nei yisrael", :gloss "of the going out of the children of Israel"} {:he "בנו משכן", :translit "banu mishkan", :gloss "they built the Tabernacle"} {:he "על מיתת אהרן", :translit "al mitat aharon", :gloss "concerning the death of Aaron"} {:he "בשנת הארבעים", :translit "bish'nat ha'arba'im", :gloss "in the fortieth year"} {:he "לצאת בני ישראל", :translit "l'tzeit b'nei yisrael", :gloss "of the going out of the children of Israel"} {:he "מיתת הצדיקים מכפרת", :translit "mitat hatzadikim m'chaperet", :gloss "the death of the righteous atones"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["לצאת" "לצאת בני ישראל" "לצאת בני ישראל"]}], :frame {:text "The death of the righteous atones, as stated by Rabbi Hiyya bar Abin in the name of Rabbi Yochanan (Mo'ed Katan 28a). This principle is linked to the deaths of Aaron and Miriam, where the verse signals their passing through language of departure (Mo'ed Katan 28a).", :sources ["Mo'ed Katan 28a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ביום הזה באו מדבר סיני", :translit "bayom hazeh ba'u midbar sinai", :gloss "On this day they came to the wilderness of Sinai"} {:he "תעבדון את האלהים על ההר הזה", :translit "ta'avdun et ha'elohim al hahar hazeh", :gloss "you shall serve Elohim on this mountain"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Verbal linkage: the day of arrival at Sinai is identified with the day God promised Moses that the people would serve Him on this mountain (Exodus 3:12)."}], :frame {:text "God promised Moses at the burning bush: 'When you bring the people out of Egypt, you shall serve God on this mountain' (Exodus 3:12). The Ba'al HaTurim reads Exodus 19:1 as the fulfillment of that promise — Israel's arrival at Sinai on that very day is the moment pledged at the burning bush.", :sources ["Exodus 3:12"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Halakhic analogy: Israel's three-month wait parallels the convert's waiting period.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["לצאת" "לצאת בני ישראל" "לצאת בני ישראל"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "לצאת", :refs ["Exodus 19:1" "Numbers 33:38" "Deuteronomy 31:2" "Judges 20:14" "Judges 20:28" "1 Samuel 23:13" "2 Samuel 13:39" "1 Kings 6:1"], :n 12, :lens :exact} {:phrase "לצאת בני ישראל", :refs ["Exodus 19:1" "Numbers 33:38" "1 Kings 6:1"], :n 3, :lens :exact} {:phrase "לצאת בני ישראל", :refs ["Exodus 19:1" "Numbers 33:38" "1 Kings 6:1"], :n 3, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "Verbal linkage: the day of arrival at Sinai is identified with the 